# security.txt — RFC 9116. The TEMPLATE for the Phase 2 address.
#
# Published since 6 October 2026 as frontend/public/.well-known/security.txt,
# with a temporary contact (the project lead's own address, their choice). This
# file is the shape it takes once a domain and a dedicated security@ exist.
#
# docs/07-security.md §10: "security.txt and a disclosure address, with a named
# person who answers it. Someone will find something after launch; the
# question is whether they can tell us."
#
# To publish:
#   1. Replace the two <…> placeholders below. The address must be read by a
#      named person — an unread inbox is worse than none, because the reporter
#      believes they have told us.
#   2. Save as frontend/public/.well-known/security.txt (Next serves /public
#      at the site root).
#   3. Set Expires to no more than a year ahead, and put the renewal in a
#      calendar: an expired file tells researchers the contact is stale.
#
# This file lives outside public/ on purpose, so a placeholder can never be
# served by accident.

Contact: mailto:<security@your-domain>
Expires: 2027-09-29T00:00:00.000Z
Preferred-Languages: en, fr
Canonical: https://<your-domain>/.well-known/security.txt
